---
import CodeBlock from '../CodeBlock.astro';
import StepCard from '../StepCard.astro';
---

<div class="sec" id="s3">
  <div class="num">03</div>
  <h2>Install Claude Code</h2>
  <p class="lede">
    The terminal looks unfriendly. You only need a few commands.
  </p>
  <p>
    A terminal is a window for typing commands. On a Mac, open Terminal using
    Spotlight. On Windows, open PowerShell from Start. The steps here cover
    those two systems; Linux users should follow the linked installation page.
  </p>
  <p>
    Claude Code needs internet access and an eligible account. Claude Pro or Max
    can be used for this exercise; the free Claude plan cannot. Check the
    account requirements on <a href="https://code.claude.com/docs/en/setup"
      >Claude Code's setup page</a
    >
    before paying. I would use a subscription here rather than set up separately billed
    developer access.
  </p>
  <div class="steps">
    <StepCard number="1" title={'Install and check it'}>
      <p>
        On the setup page, check that your computer meets the requirements. Copy
        the install command for macOS or Windows PowerShell into your terminal
        and press Enter. This downloads and runs Anthropic's installer, so take
        it from that official page, not an AI reply.
      </p>
      <p>
        When it finishes, open a new terminal window and run the check below. A
        version number means the installation worked. If it says the command was
        not found, stop here and use the installation troubleshooting link on
        the setup page.
      </p>
    </StepCard>
  </div>
  <CodeBlock
    label={'Terminal: check installation'}
    code={'claude --version'}
    copy
  />
  <div class="steps">
    <StepCard number="2" title={'Make and enter your folder'}>
      <p>
        In Finder on a Mac or File Explorer on Windows, make a new folder called
        <code class="i">my-site</code>. In the terminal, type <code class="i"
          >cd</code
        >
        followed by a space. On a Mac, drag the folder into the window. On Windows,
        copy its full location from File Explorer's address bar and paste it between
        straight double quotes. Press Enter. This changes which folder the terminal
        is working in.
      </p>
    </StepCard>
    <StepCard number="3" title={'Start and sign in'}>
      <p>
        Run the command below. The extra words select Manual mode, which asks
        before most actions that change files or run commands. Follow the
        browser sign-in prompts with your Claude account. If asked to trust a
        folder, check it is your new <code class="i">my-site</code> folder.
      </p>
    </StepCard>
  </div>
  <CodeBlock
    label={'Terminal: start with manual permissions'}
    code={'claude --permission-mode default'}
    copy
  />
  <h3>Give it standing instructions</h3>
  <p>
    <code class="i">CLAUDE.md</code> is a plain text file of project instructions
    Claude Code reads at the start of a session. In Claude Code, type "Create CLAUDE.md
    with exactly the following text, and change nothing else", then paste this block
    into the same message. Approve that file creation.
  </p>
  <CodeBlock
    label={'Example CLAUDE.md'}
    code={"# About this project\n\nThis is NZB's one page personal website.\n\n# Rules\n\nShow me a short plan and wait for my ok before changing files.\nExplain things briefly in plain English. I am not a programmer.\nDo not add anything I did not ask for.\nDo not modify the photos folder or work outside this project.\nDo not upload or publish files, install software or change permission settings without asking me."}
    copy
  />
  <p>
    Ask it to show the saved file so you can check it. These are instructions,
    not enforced restrictions. Keep Manual mode selected and approve individual
    actions rather than choosing "don't ask again". File reads may happen
    without a prompt. See <a
      href="https://code.claude.com/docs/en/permission-modes"
      >permission modes</a
    >
    if the label is unclear.
  </p>
</div>
